{"id": "b2a7c535-8828-4d67-ad22-88e159bbf878", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:54:42.893692", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "71e19233-246c-4904-aad8-bab3f81c5095", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "No network, no GPU, 224 CPUs / 2TB RAM. Let me check a few more locations for hidden repos, caches, or datasets.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "So:\n- 224 CPUs, 2TB RAM — big CPU machine, no GPU, no network.\n- No torch, transformers, numpy — nothing ML.\n- No QuaRot repo, no Phi-3 model, no datasets.\n\nThe workspace is design-only. The track.md says \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\nGiven the researcher's request is to produce a plan (stage=plan) with three blocks:\n1) Inspection of the input: what's in /workspace, whether QuaRot code/scripts/configs exist, notes about Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct or library versions. What's actually available and what's not.\n2) Proposed experiment approach (draft only): how to compare RTN vs GPTQ at 4/6/8 bit with QuaRot on Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct — metrics (perplexity, zero-shot accuracy), datasets/benchmarks, RTN/GPTQ pipeline (e.g., QuaRot-GPTQ and QuaRot-RTN from configs), baseline (FP16 or INT8/FP8 reference), controls (seeds, same calibration data, same sequences).\n3) Assumptions and uncertainties that affect design: what's unknown about resources/runtime, what determines calibration set size, how Phi-3 differs from LLaMA architecturally (attention/rotary), whether the code supports 6-bit at all, where to get zero-shot benchmarks (lm-eval-harness?), and how valid this is without GPU. Enumerate these to close them explicitly.\n\nI should give this in Russian since the researcher writes in Russian.\n\nNow, I should carefully inspect what QuaRot actually is to make the plan credible. Let me recall QuaRot (NeurIPS 2024) — \"QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\". It's a quantization method that rotates LLM hidden states using random Hadamard matrices to remove outliers, then quantizes weights+activations to 4-bit. QuaRot supports GPTQ weight quantization, per-head activation quantization (Hadamard), KV cache quantization, and optionally weight RTN (round-to-nearest). \n\nKey source facts about QuaRot repo (github spcl/QuaRot):\n- Based on a fork of GPTQ (AutoGPTQ-like) and HuggingFace transformers.\n- `quantize.py` — main entry: `--model` (HF path), `--dataset` (e.g. c4, wikitext2, ptb), `--nsamples`, `--seed`, `--rotate` (bool), `--perhead`, `--a_bits`/`--a_asym`/`--a_groupsize`, `--v_bits`, `--k_bits`, `--wbits`, `--w_asym`, `--w_groupsize`, `--load`, `--save`.\n- The repo supports weight quantization via GPTQ (`--wbits 4 --w_groupsize 128`) and also RTN mode. Actually, QuaRot's quantize.py historically quantized both weights and activations. There's a `--rtn` flag? Let me recall. Actually in QuaRot, weight quantization is GPTQ-based; there's also a variant with `qweight` RTN in `quant_linear.py` (QuantLinear supports rtn). Hmm.\n\nActually let me recall the QuaRot codebase more concretely:\n- `src/datautils.py` — get_loaders for c4, wikitext2, ptb, maybe others.\n- `src/quantize.py` — Quantizer, quantize_model, etc.\n- `qlinear.py` — QuantLinear.\n- `hada.py` — Hadamard utils.\n- `modelutils.py` — loading.\n- `quantize.py` (root) — main entrypoint: args: --model, --dataset, --nsamples, --seed, --rotate, --perhead, --a_bits, --a_asym, --a_groupsize, --v_bits, --v_asym, --v_groupsize, --k_bits, --k_asym, --k_groupsize, --wbits, --w_asym, --w_groupsize, --load, --save, --quantizer (gptq or rt_n? I think there is a `--quantizer` arg with choices ['gptq', 'rtn']). Yes! QuaRot's quantize.py has `--quantizer {gptq,rtn}` I believe. Actually I recall the \"rtn\" weight quantizer option exists in QuaRot since they compare round-to-nearest vs GPTQ. Let me confirm: In QuaRot the README says \"The default quantizer is GPTQ. ... You can also use round-to-nearest with --quantizer rt_n\". Hmm, I'm not 100% sure of the exact flag name (could be `--quantizer {rtn, gptq}` or `--rtn`). \n\nAlso there's evaluation with lm-evaluation-harness: `eval/interact.py` or scripts `eval_lm.py`, `eval_zs.py` using lm-eval-harness. The repo has `scripts/` folder with things like `run_llama2_7b_4bit_768.sh`, and `eval/get_ppl.py`, etc.\n\nAlso QuaRot supports 6-bit: GPTQ weight quantization supports arbitrary wbits (2-8 typically), so 6-bit is supported since the quantizer is bit-width generic. Groupsize configurable.\n\nPhi-3-mini-4k-instruct architecture: it's a \"Phi3\" model in transformers with 3.8B params, 32 layers, hidden 3072, 32 heads (multi-head attention), head_dim 96, rope_theta = 10000.0, sliding window = 2047 (4k context? Actually 4k-context uses sliding window attention partially). Important: Phi-3 uses `Phi3ForCausalLM`, with `Phi3Attention` (grouped-query attention NOT used in mini — actually mini uses MHA with 32 heads). It uses rotary embeddings (Phi3RotaryEmbedding) — same RoPE concept as LLaMA but with different head_dim and theta. Also Phi-3 has no bias? It does have certain differences.\n\nQuaRot generalization concerns for non-LLaMA:\n- QuaRot's modelutils uses HuggingFace Transformers AutoModelForCausalLM and monkey-patches modules by type. It supports LLaMA/LlamaForCausalLM primarily but can generalize via generic `*Attention` patch — actually QuaRot patches attention module generically (it uses `transformer_engine` or custom?). Let me recall: QuaRot's `qlinear.py`/`rotate.py` finds Linear modules by attribute names; it may have hardcoded LLaMA-specific handling (e.g., `rotary_emb`, `q_proj/k_proj/v_proj`). Also QuaRot needs the model to have \"layers\" and specific attribute names.\n- For Phi-3, there could be issues with the sliding-window attention (Phi-3 uses `attention_mask` with sliding window in Phi3ForCausalLM), and with `head_dim` different from LLaMA (96 vs 128).\n- Also, QuaRot's hadamard rotation for attention query/key uses `n_heads` and `head_dim`; Phi-3 head_dim = 96 = not a power of 2... QuaRot uses a specific Hadamard size. QuaRot truncates/embeds Hadamard to the hidden size which for LLaMA is 4096 (power of 2). Phi-3 hidden size 3072 (not power of 2!). That's a significant technical hurdle: QuaRot needs Hadamard matrices of size equal to hidden dimension; for sizes that are not a power of two, it uses the \"block-diagonal Hadamard\" or finds the nearest power-of-2 and repeats. Actually QuaRot handles non-power-of-two by composing two Hadamards (they have `get_hada_mat` with support for arbitrary N by truncating to nearest power-of-2 and stacking). This is a real uncertainty to list.\n\nZero-shot benchmarks: lm-evaluation-harness with tasks like ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, Winogrande, PIQA, OpenBookQA, BoolQ, WinoGrande. Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct is an instruct model so zero-shot eval is done in instruct-style with templates; lm-eval-harness can use openai-style chat templates.\n\nNow, on the environment: no GPU, no network, no torch. So the plan should be a design document; the actual run would require setting up environments, downloading the QuaRot repo, installing torch etc. Given \"network disabled\" and \"design-only task; repositories and compute are not required,\" the deliverable is a design/plan, not an executed experiment.\n\nGiven the researcher asked for a draft plan only (stage=plan), I need to produce:\n1) Inspection findings\n2) Draft approach\n3) Assumptions/uncertainties\n\nThen wait for approval before the full design (stage=result).\n\nActually, the instructions say: use end_researcher_turn with stage=plan to propose an approach, consequential choices and uncertainties. Wait for explicit researcher approval before developing the full design. After approval, hand off stage=result.\n\nAlso question_budget = 6 (the researcher may ask clarifying questions), actor_step_budget = 60.\n\nThe researcher gave 3 explicit blocks. Let me also verify a couple more things in the environment to strengthen the inspection block: check git availability, whether any datasets exist anywhere, check /root, /opt, /srv, /var. Network disabled confirmed via urllib (the traceback shows it failed). Let me confirm git and disk.\n\nLet me also double check there's no pip cache or conda.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "ls -la /opt /srv /var/lib 2>/dev/null | head -40; echo \"---GIT---\"; git --version 2>&1; echo \"---CONDA---\"; ls /opt/conda /usr/local 2>/dev/null; echo \"---CACHE---\"; ls -la /root/.cache /home/*/.cache 2>/dev/null || echo \"no caches\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-6d2e2dd804684564a7c347e920ca65bc", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-6d2e2dd804684564a7c347e920ca65bc", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"ls -la /opt /srv /var/lib 2>/dev/null | head -40; echo \\\"---GIT---\\\"; git --version 2>&1; echo \\\"---CONDA---\\\"; ls /opt/conda /usr/local 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---CACHE---\\\"; ls -la /root/.cache /home/*/.cache 2>/dev/null || echo \\\"no caches\\\"\", \"summary\": \"Check for repos, git, conda, caches\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-fdaf054d1c7448be81d8e2a67a1c9976", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Check for repos, git, conda, caches", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
