{"id": "5f61c7d6-dffb-44fc-b72a-9ccf7438970f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:55:40.530692", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "09151639-af66-4f75-a6e4-3396d63b6ffa", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Инспекция завершена. Фиксирую находки и передаю черновик подхода (трёхблочный план).\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "CUDA dir appears empty/just a dir, no nvcc. So no functioning GPU toolchain. This confirms: this is a design-only environment. Nothing is installed; no repos; no network; no GPU.\n\nNow I have enough for the inspection block. Let me also recall QuaRot details accurately for the plan.\n\nQuaRot (spcl/QuaRot, NeurIPS 2024): \"Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\". Main components:\n- RMSNorm-based rotation into the coordinate system of random Hadamard matrix eliminates activation outliers in both weights and activations; enables 4-bit weight+activation quantization.\n- Applies online rotation to: layer inputs (before each transformer block, via `rotate` on the module), attention output projection, MLP output projection, and also rotates the query/key/value projections per head to enable KV-cache quantization.\n- Repo: based on a fork of GPTQ-for-LLaMa by IST-DASLab, and uses HuggingFace transformers.\n- Key files (as I recall): `quantize.py` (top-level CLI), `src/` with `datautils.py`, `modelutils.py`, `qlinear.py`/`quant_linear.py`, `hada.py` (Hadamard), `rotate_llama.py` (contains rotation logic and quantizers: `Quantizer`, `gptq`, `rtn`), plus `scripts/` with `.sh` scripts (e.g., `run_llama2_7b_4bit_768.sh`, `run_llama2_13b_4bit_768.sh`), and `eval/` folder with `get_ppl.py`, `evaluate_zeroshot` (wraps lm-eval-harness), maybe `interact.py`.\n\nLet me be careful about exact flag names:\n- `--wbits` (weight bits), `--groupsize` (weight groupsize), `--a_bits`, `--v_bits`, `--k_bits` (activation/attention), `--nsamples` (calibration size), `--seed`, `--rotate`, `--perhead`, `--quantizer {gptq,rt_n}` — I believe there is indeed a `--quantizer` argument with options `gptq` and `rt_n`. Actually let me think. In QuaRot's quantize.py, I recall the argument list includes `--quantizer` choices. Hmm. I want to avoid asserting exact flag names as facts given I can't verify (no network). I'll flag these as \"to be confirmed against the pinned repo commit\" in uncertainties.\n\nActually the more I think, QuaRot has:\n- `python quantize.py --model meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf --dataset c4 --nsamples 768 --rotate --perhead --wbits 4 --a_bits 4 --v_bits 4 --k_bits 4 --save ...`\n- And weight-only GPTQ is done via `--wbits 4 --groupsize 128` with the GPTQ quantizer; RTN is available.\n\nFor 6-bit: GPTQ quantizer supports arbitrary bits (2..8), groupsize 128 or 256 or -1. So 6-bit is supported by the quantizer logic but not covered by the paper's headline experiments; needs empirical confirmation.\n\nPhi-3-mini-4k-instruct facts (from HF model card / transformers config):\n- Architecture: `Phi3ForCausalLM` (added in transformers ~4.40/4.41).\n- 3.8B params, 32 hidden layers, hidden_size 3072, intermediate 8192, num_attention_heads 32, num_key_value_heads 32 (MHA, not GQA), head_dim 96.\n- rope_theta = 10000.0, max_position_embeddings 4096, original_max_position_embeddings 4096.\n- Uses \"Phi3RotaryEmbedding\" (RoPE with complex rope + partial rotary? Actually Phi3 uses standard rotary but with `head_dim` param; uses `long_factor`/`short_factor` for RoPE scaling in newer versions — Phi-3-mini-4k uses `rope_scaling` absent for 4k? The 4k version has no RoPE scaling; it uses `original_max_position_embeddings=4096`).\n- Sliding window attention: Phi-3 (especially 4k) uses sliding window with window_size 2047 in a subset of layers (in the HF implementation `sliding_window` config, applied in layers where (layer_idx+1) % 2 == 0? Actually for Phi-3, sliding window is applied in all layers for 4k? Let me recall: For Phi-3-small, sliding window in every 2nd layer. For Phi-3-mini, I think sliding window applies to all layers except the first two? Hmm — HF config: `sliding_window: 2047` and `model_type: phi3`. In Phi3ForCausalLM, `if hasattr(config,'sliding_window') and layers_to_fuse...` Actually simpler: Phi3Attention uses `config.sliding_window` in attention mask creation when `self.layer_past` ... For mini-4k, the HF implementation uses sliding window in all 32 layers? I recall Phi-3-mini-4k has sliding window everywhere since context=4k < 2*2047. To be safe I'll list \"sliding-window attention in Phi3, window 2047, applied in HF implementation — interaction with QuaRot KV-cache rotation needs verification\" as an uncertainty.\n- Tokenizer: `Phi3Tokenizer` (actually LlamaTokenizerFast / SPM-based), uses `<|endoftext|>`, chat template with `<|user|>`, `<|assistant|>`.\n- Phi-3 differs from LLaMA: uses `Phi3ForCausalLM` class name, different module attribute names (`self.model.layers[i].self_attn.q_proj` vs LLaMA's same — actually similar naming), but: no `rotary_emb` in layer module directly (Phi3 does `self.rotary_emb = Phi3RotaryEmbedding(...)` inside Phi3Attention). Hidden size 3072 not power of two — matters for Hadamard. head_dim 96 (vs 128). Uses `Phi3SuScaledRotaryEmbedding` for long-context variants (not for 4k-mini). Grouped-query: mini uses MHA so no GQA handling needed.\n\nQuaRot generalization to non-LLaMA: The repo's rotation code in `rotate_llama.py` is LLaMA-specific (e.g., finds `self_attn.q_proj`, etc. — actually those attribute names are the same in Phi3). The main risk: hidden size 3072 (non power-of-two Hadamard), head_dim 96, sliding window attention mask (attention mask modification in Phi3Attention differs from LlamaAttention — QuaRot patches attention to use fused kernels and custom mask). Also `Phi3ForCausalLM` may not have `model.embed_tokens` — it does have `model.embed_tokens`. Naming is close to LLaMA. Also the `_set_gradient_checkpointing`/`lm_head` present.\n\nNow, for the eval: QuaRot's `eval/evaluate_zeroshot.py` uses lm-evaluation-harness tasks: truthfulqa_mc2? The README mentions running `python eval/evaluate_zeroshot.py --model ... --tasks ...` with tasks like `arc_easy, arc_challenge, hellaswag, piqa, winogrande, openbookqa, boolq`. Perplexity via `eval/get_ppl.py` with `--dataset wikitext2` etc.\n\nBaselines: QuaRot paper compares against RTN (weight-only RTN), GPTQ (weight-only GPTQ), QuaRot (rotation + per-head). The task specifically asks: compare RTN vs GPTQ in perplexity and zero-shot accuracy across 4/6/8-bit, on Phi-3 via QuaRot. So the design: \n- Model: microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (fp16/bf16 base). Note instruct model uses chat formatting for zero-shot.\n- QuaRot variants: (W-only) RTN at {4,6,8} bits; GPTQ at {4,6,8} bits. Optionally with weights-only vs with-rotation. The task says \"RTN and GPTQ\" under QuaRot — so 2 quantizers × 3 bit-widths = 6 conditions, plus baseline FP16 (and maybe INT8/FP8 reference).\n- Metrics: WikiText-2 / C4 perplexity (sliding-window / sequence-stride), plus zero-shot accuracy on lm-eval tasks (ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, BoolQ) → mean accuracy.\n- Calibration: same 128/768 samples from C4 (or same subset, same seed) for GPTQ; RTN doesn't need calibration but uses same data pipeline for fairness.\n- Controls: fixed seed, same calibration samples for both RTN and GPTQ, same eval sequences/stride, groupsize fixed (e.g., 128) across bits for both quantizers, same lm-eval worker setup, same tokenizer.\n- Baseline: FP16 full precision as reference; optionally INT8 (RTN 8 or torch.int8) / FP8 reference per QuaRot paper conventions.\n\nDesign considerations:\n- Since no GPU and no network here, the design must include an environment bootstrap section describing pip installs (torch matching CUDA, transformers version supporting Phi3, quaro pinned commit), model download from HF, datasets download — these are for the target environment, not this sandbox.\n- CPU-only fallback: given 224 cores / 2TB RAM, it's conceivable to run fp16 forward on CPU for a 3.8B model for eval (slow but feasible for small eval subsets), but GPTQ calibration on CPU would be very slow. Should note this as a fallback validity question. Actually 2TB RAM and 224 CPUs is plenty for CPU inference of 3.8B fp16 (about 7.6GB weights). CPU perplexity eval of wikitext2 (a few hundred sequences) is doable but slow. GPTQ calibration of 128 samples on CPU would take a long time. So the plan should note GPU is strongly preferred, CPU fallback only for perplexity sanity.\n\nPerplexity computation: use group size stride 512 vs 2048? WikiText-2 typically stride 512. Note that Phi-3 4k context; use stride consistent with paper (usually seq len 2048, stride 512 for Llama). Standard.\n\nNow let me structure the plan response in Russian, three blocks as requested. Then end_researcher_turn(stage=plan).\n\nLet me make sure I keep it a draft, not full design. Three blocks:\n\nBlock 1 — Инспекция входа (что реально есть/нет):\n- /workspace содержит только task.json (+ track.md reference in context? Actually track.md is in documents, not on disk). No code.\n- Нет QuaRot-репозитория, нет scripts/RTN/GPTQ, нет конфигов, нет заметок про Phi-3 и версии библиотек.\n- Нет модели Phi-3, нет датасетов, нет HuggingFace-кэша.\n- Python 3.14.4, system Python; нет torch/transformers/numpy/datasets/accelerate.\n- Есть CUD-тулчейн? /usr/local/cuda есть как директория, но nvcc отсутствует, /dev/nvidia* нет → GPU-рантайм недоступен (нет драйвера). Хотя nvidia-smi упомянут (команда есть?), но \"failed... could not communicate with NVIDIA driver\".\n- Нет сети (URLError), pip не сможет качать.\n- Ресурсы CPU: 224 ядра, 2TiB RAM.\n- Итог: среда чистая, design-only; всё для фактического запуска (код QuaRot, torch, transformers, модель, датасеты, GPU) пришлось бы поднимать в целевой среде; здесь только проектирование/документ.\n\nBlock 2 — Черновик подхода (draft):\n- Цель: сравнить RTN vs GPTQ при wbits ∈ {4,6,8} на Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct внутри пайплайна QuaRot; метрики: PPL (wikitext2, c4) + zero-shot accuracy (lm-eval-harness: arc_easy/arc_challenge/hellaswag/piqa/winogrande/openbookqa/boolq).\n- Условия: 2 квантизатора × 3 битности = 6 ячеек (+бейзлайны: FP16, INT8/FP8 reference). \n- Пайплайн: quantize.py QuaRot с --rotation (on/off optional), --quantizer gptq|rtn, --wbits, --groupsize fixed 128, --nsamples (калибровка 128/256, тот же сэмпл для обоих), --seed.\n- GPTQ: калибровка на c4 (или wikitext2) с одинаковыми сэмплами; RTN: калибровка не нужна, но используем те же данные/seed для консистентности.\n- Бейзлайн: FP16 (неквантованный) как reference; отдельно 8-бит (RTN-8 и GPTQ-8 уже в решётке; для INT8-референса можно --wbits 8 с groupsize -1 или torch native).\n- Контроли: фикс. seed, одинаковые calibration sequences, одинаковые eval sequences/stride (напр. 512), одинаковый groupsize, одинаковый tokenizer/eval harness, фикс. версии torch/transformers/quarot commit, одинаковые generation settings для instruct-формата (chat template для zero-shot).\n- Доп. валидация: QuaRot-ротация (--rotate --perhead) включена для обоих методов, чтобы честно отвечать \"QuaRot поверх Phi-3\"; также сравнить с бумарными значениями LLaMA-2 7B как контроль качества процедуры.\n- План-матрица метрик и таблица результатов.\n\nActually careful: the question is \"how do RTN and GPTQ compare\" — so the matrix is central. Also possibly include weight-only vs weight+act? The question mentions QuaRot over Phi-3. Keep it focused: RTN vs GPTQ at 4/6/8 bit. But 6-bit in QuaRot's headline is not in paper — flag.\n\nBlock 3 — Допущения и неопределённости (нужно закрыть):\n1. Ресурсы/рантайм: нет GPU и сети здесь; тайминги GPTQ-калибровки на CPU (224 ядра/2TiB) очень медленные; валидность CPU-инференса для 3.8B fp16 — ок для PPL, спорно для больших zero-shot батчей. → нужен GPU (напр., одна L40/A100 40GB) и сеть для скачивания кода/модели/датасетов.\n2. Размер калибровочного сета: в QuaRot/GPTQ по умолчанию 128 samples (nsamples 128) из C4; увел